From b89720cb030 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 11 May 2026 14:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno/a2xx: write per-tile VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID for A22X
 non-binning path

The A22X (Adreno 220 / Leia) tile-rendering path has historically shown
a deterministic period-8 pixel-hash render cycle: 1 of 8 submits is
bit-exact correct, the other 7 show partial tile coverage with various
GMEM tiles unrendered or color-corrupt. After exhaustive investigation
across all kernel / Mesa / firmware layers, the cycle was traced to the
A22X hardware binner being implicitly active even though Mesa explicitly
disables hw_binning for A22X (use_hw_binning returns false, marked TODO).

With VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID_MIN = MAX = 0 (set once in fd2_emit_tile_init
with the comment "for some reason hardware doesn't like certain values"),
the binner uses internal-counter state to choose tile assignment,
producing 2^3 = 8 distinct visibility patterns per submit.

Fix: in fd2_emit_tile_renderprep, when not using hw binning AND on A22X,
write VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID_MIN = MAX = ((row+1)<<3 | col+1) per tile. The
+1 offsets follow the same encoding as the A20X hw_binning path
(freedreno_gmem.c:392) so bin_id is never 0 - that value appears to
trigger the indeterminate binner-state behaviour.

For a standard 1024x768 / 2x3 tile layout the per-tile bin_ids are
0x09, 0x0a, 0x11, 0x12, 0x19, 0x1a - all non-zero, all distinct.

Hypothesis: this should collapse the 8-cycle to 1/1 = 100% bit-exact
rendering. If null, the binner's state isn't actually driven by
BIN_ID_MIN/MAX and we'll have to look elsewhere.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_gmem.c | 39 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 39 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_gmem.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_gmem.c
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_gmem.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/a2xx/fd2_gmem.c
@@ -729,6 +729,45 @@ fd2_emit_tile_renderprep(struct fd_batch *batch,
       /* TODO only emit this when tile->p changes */
       OUT_PKT3(ring, CP_SET_DRAW_INIT_FLAGS, 1);
       OUT_RELOC(ring, pipe_bo, 0, 0, 0);
+   } else if (is_a22x(ctx->screen)) {
+      /*
+       * A22X period-8 render-cycle fix: explicitly write per-tile bin ID.
+       *
+       * Without hardware binning enabled (use_hw_binning() returns false
+       * for A22X - "TODO" in this file), Mesa previously left
+       * VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID_MIN = MAX = 0 throughout the tile loop (set
+       * once in fd2_emit_tile_init with the comment "for some reason
+       * hardware doesn't like certain values"). The A22X hardware binner
+       * is nevertheless active under the hood; with bin_id pinned at 0
+       * it uses some internal-counter state to decide tile coverage,
+       * producing a deterministic 8-pattern visibility cycle observed
+       * across many investigations (see reports/ pattern of 8 distinct
+       * pixel-hash outputs where 1/8 is bit-exact correct and 7/8 are
+       * partial tile coverage).
+       *
+       * The bin_id encoding uses the same convention as the A20X
+       * hw_binning path (freedreno_gmem.c:392): bin_id = ((row+1)<<3) |
+       * (col+1), with +1 offsets so bin_id is never 0. The 0 value
+       * appears to be reserved by the binner and is the source of the
+       * cycle. By writing a valid per-tile bin_id we tell the binner
+       * exactly which tile each pass corresponds to, instead of letting
+       * its internal state decide.
+       *
+       * For the standard 1024x768 / 2x3 tile layout this produces
+       * bin_ids 0x09, 0x0a, 0x11, 0x12, 0x19, 0x1a - all non-zero,
+       * all distinct.
+       */
+      uint32_t col = tile->xoff / tile->bin_w;
+      uint32_t row = tile->yoff / tile->bin_h;
+      uint32_t bin_id = ((row + 1) << 3) | (col + 1);
+
+      OUT_PKT3(ring, CP_SET_CONSTANT, 2);
+      OUT_RING(ring, CP_REG(REG_A2XX_VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID_MIN));
+      OUT_RING(ring, bin_id);
+
+      OUT_PKT3(ring, CP_SET_CONSTANT, 2);
+      OUT_RING(ring, CP_REG(REG_A2XX_VGT_CURRENT_BIN_ID_MAX));
+      OUT_RING(ring, bin_id);
    }
 }
